        "original_id": "83b6fea4-cd6d-5ed6-85f3-390d63f90796",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Bride of Frankenstein, Frankenstein\n- Companies: Universal Pictures\n- People: James Whale, Peggy Webling, Mary Shelley\n- Topics: film sequels, horror, science fiction\n\nFacts:\n- In 1931, Universal Pictures released Frankenstein, an American pre-Code horror monster film directed by James Whale.\n- Frankenstein was adapted from Peggy Webling's play, which was based on Mary Shelley's novel of the same name.\n- Frankenstein follows a scientist and his assistant who assemble a man from corpses and animate him with electricity, but the assistant gives the creature an abnormal murderer's brain.\n- In 1935, Universal Pictures released Bride of Frankenstein, advertised as The Bride of Frankenstein, as an American science-fiction horror film.\n- Bride of Frankenstein was the first sequel to Universal Pictures' 1931 hit Frankenstein.\n- Bride of Frankenstein is widely considered one of the few sequels to a great film that is even better than the original.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "61e487be-c35e-5006-b3f3-f2cd2ce0feff",
        "text": "Page: Bride of Frankenstein. Sentence: Bride of Frankenstein (advertised as The Bride of Frankenstein) is a 1935 American science-fiction horror film, the first sequel to Universal Pictures' 1931 hit \"Frankenstein\".\nPage: Bride of Frankenstein. Sentence: It is considered one of the few sequels to a great film that is even better than the original film on which it is based.\nPage: Frankenstein (1931 film). Sentence: Frankenstein is a 1931 American pre-Code horror monster film from Universal Pictures directed by James Whale and adapted from the play by Peggy Webling (which in turn is based on the novel of the same name by Mary Shelley), about a scientist and his assistant who dig up corpses to build a man animated by electricity, but his assistant accidentally gives the creature an abnormal, murderer's brain.",
